I have everything needed. Here is the detailed reconnaissance map.

---

# Reconnaissance Report

Note upfront: `analytics/history.json` does **not** live in this repo. Per `CLAUDE.md:49` and the `path.resolve("analytics", "history.json")` calls (cwd-relative), it lives in the sibling `agile-pain-relief-astro` repo. So the code that reads/writes it is here, but the data file itself is not.

## package.json scripts (`/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/package.json`)

```
test           → vitest run
export         → npx tsx buffer-export-via-playwright/bufferExport.ts   (LinkedIn only, TS version)
scrape         → node buffer-export-via-playwright/scrapeAll.mjs        (all 3 platforms — THE scraper)
post           → npx tsx buffer-post/bufferPost.ts                      (single post via GraphQL)
batch          → npx tsx buffer-post/batchPost.ts                       (batch scheduling + probe)
calendar-audit → node calendar-audit/calendarAudit.mjs
dashboard      → npx tsx analytics/dashboard/generateDashboard.ts       (not present in this repo)
```

Important: `npm run scrape` runs **`scrapeAll.mjs`**, not `bufferExport.ts`. `bufferExport.ts` (LinkedIn-only) is the older `npm run export` path and imports from `historyStore.ts`. `scrapeAll.mjs` **inlines** its own copies of the history-merge logic (it does not import `historyStore.ts`), because it uses string-based `page.evaluate` to dodge esbuild/tsx `__name` injection issues (see comment at `scrapeAll.mjs:552-565`).

---

## 1. Playwright engagement-metrics scraper

Entry point: `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-export-via-playwright/scrapeAll.mjs`

Supporting/related files:
- `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-export-via-playwright/bufferExport.ts` — older LinkedIn-only scraper (`npm run export`), class `BufferScraper`.
- `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-export-via-playwright/historyStore.ts` — pure merge functions + I/O, imported by `bufferExport.ts` only.
- `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-export-via-playwright/mastodonExport.ts` and `blueskyExport.ts` — standalone one-off exporters that write CSV (`results/mastodon-posts.csv`); **not referenced by any script** (grep found no importers). Legacy/experimental.

### Buffer navigation (`scrapeAll.mjs`)
- `initBrowser()` (line 435): launches `chromium.launchPersistentContext` against `~/.buffer-playwright-session` (`process.env.HOME + "/.buffer-playwright-session"`), `headless: false`, viewport 1400x900. Persistent profile keeps the Buffer login.
- `navigateToChannel(page, channelId)` (line 445): goes to `https://publish.buffer.com/all-channels?channels[]=${channelId}&tab=sent`. If a "Log in" button is visible it pauses up to 300s waiting for the user to log in (`waitForURL("**/all-channels**")`).
- Channel IDs (`scrapeAll.mjs:8-12`): LinkedIn `51a55227ead9b8a169000025`, Mastodon `6a0b7565090476fb9933fe3a`, Bluesky `677c106e4697c1deff913923`.
- `scrollUntilCutoff(page)` (line 467): infinite-scroll loop (max 500 scrolls). Reads oldest visible date header via regex over `document.body.innerText`, stops once oldest visible date < `CUTOFF_DATE` (6 weeks ago, `scrapeAll.mjs:15`). Falls back to scrolling inner scrollable divs when `document.body.scrollHeight` stops growing; gives up after 3 no-progress iterations.
- `main()` (line 873) runs `processLinkedIn` → `processMastodon` → `processBluesky` sequentially, then closes context.

### Metric extraction — shared DOM harness `extractPosts(page, metricNames, filterOut)` (line 560)
Runs a **string-literal** `page.evaluate`. Logic:
- Finds every `[aria-label="Metrics list"]` region.
- Within each region, reads metric label/value pairs: label = `[class*="publish_label_"] span`, value = `:scope > [class*="publish_metric_"]` inside each `[class*="publish_wrapper_"]` (lines 579-588). Builds `metrics[label] = value`.
- Keeps a region only if it has at least one of `metricNames` and none of `filterOut` (lines 591-593).
- Walks up ≤15 parents to the post card (container whose text contains "View Post"), maps container→metrics.
- Extracts post text (longest `<p>`/text/content element that isn't a metric label / "Published via" / "View Post"), nearest date header above the card, and a nearby time (`\d{1,2}:\d{2} AM/PM`). Returns `{ text, dateHeader, time, metrics, domOrder }` sorted by vertical position.

`parseMetricValue(text)` (line 95): strips commas, handles `K`/`M` suffixes (×1000 / ×1e6), returns rounded int or `null` for `-`, `--`, empty.

### Per-platform parsing
Buffer relabeled all channels to generic **Reactions / Comments / Reposts** around 2026-06 (see comments at `scrapeAll.mjs:771-774` and `825-827`). Per-channel navigation isolates the platform; `filterOut` only guards against stray LinkedIn cards.

- **LinkedIn** — `processLinkedIn` (line 692). `extractPosts(page, ["Reactions","Comments","Impressions","Reach","Eng. Rate"], ["Favorites","Likes"])`. Builds `{ platform:"LinkedIn", postText, postedDateTime, metrics:{ reactions, comments, impressions, reach, engagementRate } }` (lines 723-738). `engagementRate` gets a `%` appended if missing. Text cleaned of "Reactions…", "Published via…", "View Post…", "see more…", "Mark Levison". buff.ly URLs expanded via `expandBuffLyUrlsInText` (HEAD-follow redirect).
- **Mastodon** — `processMastodon` (line 762). `extractPosts(page, ["Reactions","Comments","Reposts"], ["Impressions","Reach"])`. Maps generic labels to Mastodon semantics: **favorites = Reactions, replies = Comments, reblogs = Reposts** (lines 801-803). Produces `{ postedDateTime, text, favorites, replies, reblogs }`.
- **Bluesky** — `processBluesky` (line 816). Same `extractPosts` call. Maps **likes = Reactions, comments = Comments, reposts = Reposts** (lines 854-856). Produces `{ postedDateTime, text, likes, comments, reposts }`.

Dates parsed by `parseBufferDate(dayPart, timePart)` (line 33): handles "N days ago", "Saturday, January 17" + "10:29 AM", guesses year, and **validates day-of-week** to fall back to previous year (lines 71-86). `formatDateTime` → `"YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss"`.

### Matching scraped data back to history
- **LinkedIn** — `syncLinkedInToHistory` (line 182): primary match by **datetime** (`postedDateTime` → `.replace(" ","T")`, looked up in a `postsByDate` map). Fallback: `findHistoryIndexByLinkedInText` (line 168) matches by first-60-char, case-insensitive prefix of the `linkedin` field (handles `bufferPost.ts` entries that have `posted:""`); when matched it back-fills `posted`/`created`. Otherwise creates a new post. Sorts newest-first, writes.
- **Mastodon** — `syncMastodonToHistory` (line 316): groups scraped posts into **threads** (posts ≤10 min apart), sums favorites/replies/reblogs across thread parts, matches to history by the first part's text via `findHistoryIndexByText(posts, text, "mastodon")` (line 281). No datetime match — text only. Skips unmatched (does not create).
- **Bluesky** — `syncBlueskyToHistory` (line 394): per-post text match via `findHistoryIndexByText(..., "bluesky")`. Skips unmatched.
- `findHistoryIndexByText` (line 281) is two-pass: pass 1 matches the platform's own field (`post.mastodon[0]` or `post.bluesky` string); pass 2 falls back to matching `post.linkedin`. 60-char case-insensitive prefix.

`historyStore.ts` mirrors this for the LinkedIn-only `export` path: `mergeScrapedPosts` (line 231), `findMatchingPost` (datetime, line 136), `findIndexByLinkedInText` (line 149), `updateExistingPost` (line 185), `createNewHistoryPost` (line 202), `syncScrapedPostsToHistory` (line 317).

---

## 2. Buffer GraphQL API client — `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-post/bufferPost.ts`

- **Endpoint**: `const BUFFER_API_URL = "https://api.buffer.com"` (line 37).
- **Auth**: `BUFFER_API_TOKEN`. `loadEnv()` (line 49) parses `.env` manually, exits if the file or the token is missing. `bufferApiQuery` (line 103) sends `Authorization: Bearer ${token}`, `Content-Type: application/json`, POST body `{ query, variables }`. Throws on non-2xx.
- **Response type** (line 81): `interface GraphQLResponse<T> { data?: T; errors?: Array<{ message: string }> }`.

### `createPost` mutation (verbatim, `bufferPost.ts:86-101`)
```graphql
mutation CreatePost($input: CreatePostInput!) {
  createPost(input: $input) {
    ... on PostActionSuccess {
      post {
        id
        text
        status
        dueAt
      }
    }
    ... on MutationError {
      message
    }
  }
}
```

Input builders: `buildLinkedInInput` (154), `buildMastodonInput` (173), `buildBlueskyInput` (201). `CreatePostInput` shape (line 132):
```ts
interface CreatePostInput {
  channelId: string;
  text: string;
  schedulingType: "automatic" | "notification";
  mode: "addToQueue" | "shareNow" | "shareNext" | "customScheduled" | "recommendedTime";
  dueAt?: string;
  assets?: Array<{ image: { url: string } }>;
  metadata?: {
    mastodon?: { thread?: Array<{ text: string }> };
    bluesky?:  { thread?: Array<{ text: string }> };
  };
}
```
Extra thread parts (`content.mastodon.slice(1)` / `content.bluesky.slice(1)`) become `metadata.<platform>.thread`. Cover image is the blog's `og:image`, fetched by `fetchCoverImageUrl` (line 473). `main()` (line 664) posts to all three channels sequentially, then calls `createHistoryEntry`.

### Code that READS posts back from the API — YES, in `batchPost.ts`
Located in `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-post/batchPost.ts` (used by `npm run batch`, not by `post`). Two read queries, both via the same `bufferApiQuery`:

`ORG_QUERY` (line 236, verbatim):
```graphql
query { account { organizations { id } } }
```

`SCHEDULED_POSTS_QUERY` (lines 238-259, verbatim):
```graphql
query ScheduledPosts(
  $organizationId: OrganizationId!,
  $channelIds: [ChannelId!]!
) {
  posts(
    input: {
      organizationId: $organizationId,
      filter: { channelIds: $channelIds, status: [scheduled] }
    },
    first: 100
  ) {
    edges {
      node {
        id
        dueAt
        status
        channelId
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Consumed by `fetchOrganizationId` (line 261) and `probeOccupiedSlots` (line 275), which collect already-scheduled `dueAt` slots per channel so the batch scheduler avoids collisions. So the only read-back path is scheduled-post probing — there is **no** code that reads posts' *engagement metrics* back from the API (metrics come exclusively from the Playwright scraper). Note `posts(...)` uses `input:{ organizationId, filter:{ channelIds, status:[scheduled] } }` with cursor-style `edges { node {...} }`.

Tests: `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-post/bufferApi.test.ts`, `slotAllocator.test.ts`; helpers `slotAllocator.ts`, `slotPlan.mjs`/`.d.mts`.

---

## 3. history.json structure

Two slightly different `HistoryPost` type declarations exist (the scraper adds `engagement` sub-objects; the poster adds `linkedinComment`):

`historyStore.ts:31-45` (scraper/export view):
```ts
interface HistoryPost {
  id: number;
  created: string;              // "YYYY-MM-DD"
  posted: string;               // "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss" (or "" until export back-fills)
  linkedin: string | null;
  mastodon: string[] | null;
  bluesky: string | null;
  topics: string[];
  links: string[];
  engagement: Record<string, unknown>;
}
interface HistoryData { posts: HistoryPost[]; }
```

`bufferPost.ts:16-31` (poster view) is the same plus one extra field:
```ts
linkedinComment: string | null;   // legacy first-comment link text, now always null
```

Note a shape inconsistency worth flagging: `createHistoryEntry` (bufferPost.ts:642-644) writes `mastodon` as `string[]` but `bluesky` as a single **joined string** (`content.bluesky.join("\n\n---\n\n")`), while the type says `bluesky: string | null` and `mastodon: string[] | null`. The scraper's Mastodon sync stores `mastodon` as `string[]` (thread parts) and Bluesky as a plain string — consistent with those types.

### Engagement stored per platform (nested under `engagement`)
Written by `scrapeAll.mjs`:
- `engagement.linkedin` (line 202-208): `{ reactions, comments, impressions, reach, engagementRate }` (nulls coerced to `0` / `"0%"`).
- `engagement.mastodon` (line 379-382): `{ favorites, replies, reblogs }` (summed across thread parts).
- `engagement.bluesky` (line 415-419): `{ likes, comments, reposts }`.

Typed in `historyStore.ts`: `LinkedInPostMetrics` (line 8, nullable) vs `LinkedInEngagement` (line 23, non-null) — `toHistoryEngagement` (line 169) converts nulls to `0`/`"0%"`.

### I/O
- Read/write helpers in three places, all targeting `path.resolve("analytics","history.json")` (cwd-relative): `historyStore.ts:300-312` (`readHistory`/`writeHistory`/`syncScrapedPostsToHistory`), `scrapeAll.mjs:151-160`, `bufferPost.ts:611-620`. All write with `JSON.stringify(data, null, 2) + "\n"`.
- Posts are always **sorted by `posted` descending (newest first)** before writing (`scrapeAll.mjs:265`, `historyStore.ts:287`, `bufferPost.ts:652`; confirmed in `CLAUDE.md:79`).
- `createHistoryEntry` (bufferPost.ts:631) creates the entry at post time with `posted:""`; the scraper later back-fills `posted`/`created` when it matches by text prefix.

Tests for the merge logic: `/Users/marklevison/Documents/GitHub/APR/social-media-posting/buffer-export-via-playwright/historyStore.test.ts`.

